          "type": "intro",
          "title": "A Child Without a Birthday",
          "content": "He did not know the year he was born. He did not know his father. He barely knew his mother — she walked twelve miles at night, after a full day in the fields, just to lie beside him until dawn, then walked twelve miles back. He saw her, by his own count, four or five times in his life.\n\nThat child, born sometime around **February 1818** on a Maryland plantation near a creek called **Tuckahoe**, would become **Frederick Douglass** — the most photographed American of the 19th century, advisor to presidents, the conscience of a nation.\n\nBut before any of that, he was just a boy named **Frederick Augustus Washington Bailey**, owned by a man he had never met, on a farm where childhood itself was a privilege denied.\n\nThis lesson begins where Douglass began: in the **specific, granular reality of slavery on Maryland's Eastern Shore** — not slavery as abstraction, but slavery as a child experienced it, day by day. To understand the man, we have to refuse the comfort of skipping past the boy.",
