          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Maryland's Eastern Shore: The Geography of Bondage",
          "content": "Douglass was born in **Talbot County, Maryland** — the Eastern Shore of the Chesapeake Bay. This geography matters more than students often realize.\n\n**Why Maryland slavery is its own case study**:\n\n- A **border state** — slave but not Deep South. By 1810, Maryland had more **free Black people** than any other state (over 33,000), living alongside the enslaved.\n- The **tobacco economy was collapsing**. Planters were shifting to wheat, which needed less labor. Many enslaved people were **hired out**, sold south, or **manumitted** as their value to planters fell.\n- The Eastern Shore was rural, isolated, poor — but **close enough to Pennsylvania and free territory** that escape was geographically conceivable.\n\n**The plantation system Douglass was born into**:\n\n- Owned by **Aaron Anthony**, a middling overseer who managed the estate of **Colonel Edward Lloyd V**, one of the richest men in Maryland — over **1,000 acres** and **500+ enslaved people** at \"Wye House.\"\n- Douglass spent his earliest years at his grandmother **Betsy Bailey's** cabin on Tuckahoe Creek, away from the main plantation. She raised the small children for the absent mothers.\n- At about age 6, he was forced to leave Betsy and walk to **Wye House**, the great plantation. He would later write of this moment as the **first conscious wound** of slavery.",
