          "explanation": "Wells's signal contribution was **investigative statistical journalism**. She studied lynching records compiled by the Chicago Tribune and others, traveled to lynching sites, interviewed witnesses, and demonstrated that the rape accusation was raised in **fewer than one-third of cases**, and that many of those accusations themselves were fabricated or grossly distorted. The actual triggers were economic competition (her three Memphis friends were killed for running a successful grocery), Black political organizing, and pure terror. This was devastating because the white South's entire moral cover for mob murder rested on the claim that it was protecting white women. Wells **demolished that cover with numbers**. Federal anti-lynching legislation, despite repeated attempts (including the Dyer Bill of 1922), never passed during her lifetime; the U.S. Senate did not pass anti-lynching legislation until 2022, with the Emmett Till Antilynching Act.",
